# Installed by deploy/deploy.sh on every deploy (install + daemon-reload).
# Warms the RIS↔ME map of the current GP: ~46 upstream requests that must
# not land on a visitor. Started by the timer nightly
# and by deploy.sh after each restart, because the Nitro cache is in-memory.
# Since 24.09.2026 the deploy WAITS for this unit instead of firing it with
# --no-block: it is the cold minute after the restart, and a deploy that
# reports success in the middle of it hands that minute to the first visitor.
#
# Second call since 2026-09-17: the homepage now also states what is in
# Begutachtung WITHOUT a Gegenstand at Parliament.
# It reads the same corpus the first call warms, so it adds no
# upstream traffic — but it has its own 30-minute derived cache, and that
# section is a correction to a claim the page makes, so it must not be the
# one part of the start page that arrives cold. (Its path followed the
# rename to /api/ris-drafts on 2026-09-18.)
#
# Third call since 2026-09-18: the station map behind the station filter.
# This is the expensive one — 227 upstream requests for GP XXVIII,
# 650 for XXVII, 35.6 s cold — and the only one that MUST run here: the list
# reads the map under a 2.5 s budget and simply answers without stations when
# it is cold, so a map nobody warms is a filter nobody sees. --max-time 900
# rather than 600 because the timer may fire on a period twice that size.
#
# Fourth call since 2026-09-19: the Bundesgesetzblatt Teil II volumes the
# Verordnung pages read their Kundmachung from. Three volumes are
# about 21 requests to RIS when cold — and unlike the other sections, the
# result of a cold run would not merely be slow but a statement: whoever does
# not have the volumes finds no Kundmachung and would write „nicht
# kundgemacht" under a Verordnung that has been in force for a long time.
#
# It stands BEFORE the list call, ever since the list shows the outcome per
# row: the list waits only 3 s for it and otherwise renders without
# the column — which is right, but avoidable when the volumes are already in.
#
# Fifth call since 2026-09-26: the station map of the PREVIOUS period. Same
# argument as the fourth call, one period back — the list reads the map under
# a 2,5 s budget, so whoever picks GP XXVII from the period chooser on a cold
# server waits those 2,5 s and then gets a list without the station filter
# plus a note. The note is honest and the filter is the accountability layer,
# so it is the one that must not be missing: the finished period is where the
# question „was ist daraus geworden" has an answer at all.
#
# It is the expensive line — 650 upstream requests against 227 for the
# running period, 35,6 s cold — and it stands LAST because of it:
# every call before it serves a page somebody reaches without choosing
# anything, and a deploy that breaks off in the middle of this unit should
# lose the least-visited warm-up, not the start page's.
#
# `vorperiode` and not `XXVII`, for the reason `aktuell` exists
# (`server/utils/http/params.ts`): a Roman numeral in a unit file is a line
# that goes wrong on exactly the day nobody is watching it, the
# Periodenwechsel — and then it would warm the period that just ended while
# the list shows the one that just began.
#
# Sixth call since 22.10.2026: the Länder of the running period, every
# draft open and closed — what `/entwuerfe` shows once a Land is chosen, and
# what every closed Land draft page reads. Cold it is the Salzburg register
# (13 s), the Landesgesetzblatt of three Länder from RIS (about 40 requests)
# and the title search over all of it. All
# three are held stale-while-revalidate, so one warm run after the restart
# keeps them warm. It stands before the previous period's map: a Land is a
# choice too, but a cheaper one to have ready. `status=all` without `gp` is
# the current period, for the reason `aktuell` exists.
#
# MEASURED AND DELIBERATELY NOT DONE: warming
# `/api/drafts/:gp/:inr/konsolidiert` for the drafts in Begutachtung (cold
# ~12 s, warm 10 ms, day cache). Three reasons, and the first is the one that
# decides. That section is fetched lazily and client-side
# (`compare/TextComparisonSection.vue`, `server: false, lazy: true`; since
# 12.11.2026 not the only one — the sections below the fold ask on approach,
# `useNearViewportSection`): the page renders and reads without it and
# then fills in — nobody is told something false in the meantime, which is
# what separates it from the station map and the BGBl volumes above. Second,
# it is per draft and unbounded: 19 drafts were open on 26.09.2026, so a good
# four minutes added to a deploy this unit blocks by design, and it grows
# with the ME count — a deploy that gets slower every month is a deploy that
# stops being run. Third, each draft pulls every § of every law it amends
# from RIS, nightly, for a layer that is opened on a median 12 % of a draft's
# §§. The first visitor of the day pays 12 s for a section that
# says „lädt"; that is the right one to pay it.
[Unit]
Description=Begutachtungs-Monitor: warm the RIS corpus, BGBl volumes, both station maps, the genannt index, the Länder, the EU map, the laws without a draft and the homepage's Parliament doors
After=begutachtungs-monitor.service
Requires=begutachtungs-monitor.service
# Since 30.09.2026: the pages of the open drafts, in their own unit and
# without blocking (begutachtungs-monitor-prewarm-pages.service) — after the
# corpora below, which those pages read. OnSuccess= rather than an
# ExecStartPost that runs `systemctl start`: this unit runs as `app`, which
# may not start units, and `ExecStartPost=+…` would hand a root command to an
# otherwise unprivileged job. OnSuccess= queues the job from systemd itself,
# only when every ExecStart below succeeded — the condition ExecStartPost had —
# and without blocking whoever started this unit (deploy.sh, the watchdog).
OnSuccess=begutachtungs-monitor-prewarm-pages.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
# Nothing here needs root: curl against localhost.
User=app
Group=app
NoNewPrivileges=true
PrivateTmp=true
# The unit's own worst case, stated rather than inherited: the sixteen --max-time
# budgets below add up to 6.660 s, and deploy.sh blocks on this job. (Recounted
# 16.11.2026: the three EU lists of the same day had left it at „twelve" and
# 6.180 s, under the 6.540 s the lines then added up to.)
TimeoutStartSec=6740
# First, and no curl (12.11.2026): the published documents the app keeps on
# disk (server/utils/cache/documentDriver.ts) — law texts, § versions,
# Erläuterungen, closed Bundesgesetzblatt years, upstream payloads only and
# never a Stellungnahme. The driver ignores TTLs: Nitro checks the 30 days on
# every read and overwrites what has expired, so no answer is ever too old,
# but a file nobody asks for again would stay forever. This deletes what is
# older than the 30 days plus a margin — `-mtime +31` is 32 whole days, so
# nothing still valid goes. The path is the app's StateDirectory, which
# systemd always puts at /var/lib/<name> for a system unit; written out like
# the report directory in the diagnostics drop-in, because a `$` in an
# ExecStart line is systemd's expansion before it is anyone else's. The `-`
# makes a failure harmless: before the app's first write the directory does
# not exist yet, find says so in the journal, and the warm-up below still
# runs and OnSuccess= still fires. First, so a curl that fails cannot skip it.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/find /var/lib/begutachtungs-monitor/documents -type f -mtime +31 -delete
# The first request waits for the port. `After=` orders this job behind the
# app's START, not behind its LISTEN: after a hand-typed `systemctl restart`
# the curl came at 16:56:15 and Node listened at 16:56:16 (29.09.2026,
# status 7). deploy.sh only escaped it through its `sleep 2`. Ten seconds of
# connection-refused retries cost nothing when the port is already open.
ExecStart=/usr/bin/curl -fsS -o /dev/null --retry 10 --retry-connrefused --retry-delay 1 --max-time 600 http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/ris-map/aktuell
ExecStart=/usr/bin/curl -fsS -o /dev/null --max-time 300 http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/bgbl/teil2
ExecStart=/usr/bin/curl -fsS -o /dev/null --max-time 120 "http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/ris-drafts?status=open"
ExecStart=/usr/bin/curl -fsS -o /dev/null --max-time 900 http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/stations/aktuell
# Since 14.11.2026, right after the station map it reads: the running
# period's „genannt" index (server/utils/parliament/namedInRvIndex.ts) —
# which closed drafts without a Vorlage the Erläuterungen of another
# draft's Vorlage name. The draft page, the list and the homepage read it
# under a 2-s brake and say „Bisher keine Regierungsvorlage" until it is
# there. Cold it is one detail JSON per Vorlage inside a candidate's window
# (the station map has just fetched most of them) and their Erläuterungen,
# which stay on the document disk for a month; a PDF only where it could be
# the second witness. The running period only: the previous one builds on
# its first request and is held a week.
ExecStart=/usr/bin/curl -fsS -o /dev/null --max-time 600 http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/genannt/aktuell
ExecStart=/usr/bin/curl -fsS -o /dev/null --max-time 300 "http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/laender?status=all"
# The homepage's cut of the same Länder lists (30.10.2026): read right after them, it
# costs the cut and nothing upstream.
ExecStart=/usr/bin/curl -fsS -o /dev/null --max-time 120 http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/dashboard/laender
# The three lists of /eu-recht that read the Commission (16.11.2026): the
# open feedback windows of „Ihre Meinung zählt", the European Citizens'
# Initiatives in collection, the directives to transpose. None was warmed;
# each answer is kept a day, so the day's first reader paid for it — measured
# on the production build after a restart: 6.6 s, 8 to 15 s and 2.5 s, and a
# page rendered on the server waits for the slowest of its lists. Before the
# EU map, which takes minutes, because they take seconds. With a leading `-`:
# they read the Commission's services, and a portal or register that does not
# answer must not stop what follows — the map, the Vorperiode, the Länder's
# laws. A failed one is retried the next night; until then its list says
# „Gerade nicht abrufbar" to the reader, as it does today.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/curl -fsS -o /dev/null --max-time 120 http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/eu-recht/fenster
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/curl -fsS -o /dev/null --max-time 180 http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/eu-recht/buergerinitiativen
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/curl -fsS -o /dev/null --max-time 120 http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/eu-recht/umsetzung
# Seventh call since 04.11.2026: the EU map —
# which drafts of the running period implement EU law: the
# list's token and its filter „Setzt EU-Recht um", the pages under
# /eu-recht, the „Weitere Entwürfe" line under a draft's fact. After the
# station map and the Länder lists, which the map joins for a draft's stand.
# The one call here that keeps its work on disk (StateDirectory, eu-recht/):
# cold it read 156 Ministerialentwürfe, 219 RIS records and 449 Länder
# drafts in 181 s on the production build (04.11.2026, Steiermark blocked),
# heap peak 185 MB; after a restart, from the store, 33 s and nothing
# re-read. Only a draft that is new, still open or whose documents changed
# is read again, so the nightly cost is the day's arrivals. --max-time 1800:
# the Länder are paced at one request a second, and a Land that blocks is
# retried the next night, not waited for.
# Since 06.11.2026 the same call builds the Bund-zu-Land map
# (bund-land/ beside eu-recht/): the Länder run as one loop over both
# stores, so a changed draft's PDFs are read once for both facts. No line
# of its own — a second route would fetch every Land's PDFs again. The first
# build after that deploy reads all Land drafts for the new store once.
ExecStart=/usr/bin/curl -fsS -o /dev/null --max-time 1800 "http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/eu-recht/karte?gp=aktuell"
ExecStart=/usr/bin/curl -fsS -o /dev/null --max-time 900 http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/stations/vorperiode
# Ninth call since 09.11.2026: the laws of the running period without a
# Begutachtung, behind the chip „Ohne Begutachtung" on /entwuerfe. LAST on purpose: it reads both station maps
# above under a 2.5 s budget and answers `gap: 'pending'` while either is
# cold — ExecStart= lines of a oneshot run one after the other, so here
# both are warm and the answer is the six-hour one. Its own reads are the
# Teil-I halves of the Jahrgang pages the BGBl call above already fetched,
# list 81 and 101 (cached) and one list call for the period's Anträge — a
# few MB. The running period only: an older one would build a third
# station map on the 320 MB heap, and nobody needs it warm.
ExecStart=/usr/bin/curl -fsS -o /dev/null --max-time 120 http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/ohne-begutachtung
# Tenth call since 11.11.2026: the Länder's laws without a draft in their
# register — rows of /entwuerfe once a Land is chosen. After the Länder
# lists above, whose registers and title search it reads (all held
# stale-while-revalidate); its own reads are the Landesgesetzblatt indexes
# those lists already fetched and, for Salzburg, the Landtag PDFs behind the
# statement, one at a time on the process's slot. It waits 2 s for them and
# answers `pending` meanwhile, so the reads go on after this call returns —
# the list asks again on its own. Steiermark is never waited for: PALLAST is
# read at its pace by the Länder call, and a cold Steiermark answers
# „nicht abrufbar" here within its 2 s budget instead of queueing more pages.
ExecStart=/usr/bin/curl -fsS -o /dev/null --max-time 300 http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/laender/gesetze
# Twelfth call since 15.11.2026: the running period's Anliegen an den
# Nationalrat — the list /anliegen and the homepage's block. Lists 101 and
# 143, per kind and for the Petitionen an den Bundesrat, side by side; then
# one detail JSON per item at four at a time, the period's (about 70 in GP
# XXVIII) first and the Bundesrat's 42 after them, so this one call warms both
# the list and its Bundesrat tab. The endpoint waits 20 s at most for the
# whole answer and answers with what came, while the rest go on into the
# leaf cache (5 s cold with Parliament fast, 15.11.2026). 60 s covers that
# and a cold `getCurrentGp` (8 s x 3 attempts) in front of it.
ExecStart=/usr/bin/curl -fsS -o /dev/null --max-time 60 http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/anliegen
# Since 16.11.2026: the open Vorlagen and motions that take a Stellungnahme —
# the homepage's door „Stellung nehmen im Parlament" and the rows of
# /entwuerfe under „Regierungsvorlage" and „Parlament / Landtag". The
# endpoint has no cache of its own: it reads list 101 (`items-gp`), one
# detail JSON per candidate (`gegenstand`, both 30 minutes fresh and served
# up to three hours while they refresh) and each candidate's Stellungnahmen
# (`statements-rv`, 30 minutes, never served stale), plus list 81 where a
# row names no draft. Warming those leaves is what helps: about a dozen
# candidates, cold some seconds, warm milliseconds. So it pays mostly after
# a deploy, which empties every memory cache; the nightly run's Stellungnahmen
# are cold again by morning, by design, since their count is a claim about
# now. After the Anliegen, the homepage's other Parliament door, and with a
# leading `-`: a failure here must not keep OnSuccess= from rendering the
# open drafts' pages.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/curl -fsS -o /dev/null --max-time 120 http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/dashboard/zweite-runde
